CAT = "llqpLifeTax"; SEG = "LTX"
Q = [
("Rosa surrenders a whole life policy for a cash value of 47,300 dollars. Its adjusted cost basis is 31,800 dollars. How much is her taxable policy gain?",
 "15,500 dollars", ["7,750 dollars", "31,800 dollars", "47,300 dollars"],
 "The policy gain is proceeds minus ACB: 47,300 - 31,800 = 15,500. It is taxed as income, not as a capital gain, so none of it is halved."),
("A taxable policy gain on surrendering a life policy is taxed as:",
 "Regular income", ["A capital gain", "A tax-free receipt", "A dividend"],
 "Under the Income Tax Act, a gain on the disposition of a life insurance policy is fully included in income in the year of disposition."),
("A life insurance death benefit paid to a named beneficiary is:",
 "Received tax-free", ["Taxed as the beneficiary's income", "Taxed as a capital gain", "Taxed in the owner's final return"],
 "Death benefits from a life insurance policy are not taxable to the beneficiary, and death is not a disposition that creates a policy gain."),
("The net cost of pure insurance (NCPI) of a policy:",
 "Reduces the policy's ACB", ["Is deductible from the owner's income", "Raises the cash value each year", "Is added to the death benefit"],
 "The ACB is premiums paid less the cumulative NCPI (and certain other amounts). On a permanent policy the ACB often falls to zero in later years."),
("Ali takes a policy loan larger than the policy's ACB. The part above the ACB is:",
 "A taxable policy gain", ["Always fully tax-free", "An allowable capital loss", "Added to the policy's ACB"],
 "A policy loan is a disposition to the extent it exceeds the ACB. Repaying it later can produce a deduction for the amount that was taxed."),
("Lena transfers a policy on her son's life to her son. Under the Income Tax Act, the transfer:",
 "Can be made at ACB, tax-free", ["Is taxed at fair market value", "Is taxed as a gift to Lena", "Ends the policy's coverage"],
 "A policy on the life of a child or grandchild can roll over to that child tax-free at the policy's ACB, so no policy gain arises for the parent."),
("A corporation receives a 900,000 dollar death benefit on a key person policy with an ACB of 40,000 dollars. How much is credited to the capital dividend account?",
 "860,000 dollars", ["900,000 dollars", "450,000 dollars", "940,000 dollars"],
 "The CDA credit is the death benefit minus the policy's ACB: 900,000 - 40,000 = 860,000. The company can then pay that amount to shareholders as a tax-free capital dividend."),
("Premiums for a key person policy owned by and payable to the company are generally:",
 "Not deductible", ["Fully deductible", "Half deductible", "Deductible over five years"],
 "Premiums for life insurance are personal or capital outlays and generally are not deductible, even when a business owns the policy."),
("A bank requires a life policy as collateral for a business loan. The business may be able to deduct:",
 "The lesser of premium and NCPI", ["The whole premium every year", "Twice the interest on the loan", "The policy's full cash value"],
 "Where the assignment is required by the lender for a business loan, the Income Tax Act allows a deduction for the lesser of the premium paid and the NCPI for the year."),
("At death, a Canadian is deemed to have sold capital property such as a cottage at:",
 "Fair market value", ["Its original cost", "Half its value", "Its assessed property tax value"],
 "The deemed disposition at death taxes accrued capital gains unless the property passes to a spouse or common-law partner, where a rollover can defer the tax."),
("Why can naming a beneficiary on a policy reduce estate costs in most provinces?",
 "Proceeds bypass the estate and probate", ["Probate charges are halved", "The policy avoids the deemed disposition", "Beneficiaries pay the probate fees"],
 "Proceeds paid to a named beneficiary go directly to them, outside the estate, so they are not subject to probate fees and are paid faster. Rules on probate vary in Quebec."),
("A family needs 30,000 dollars a year forever after the earner dies. At a 5% return, how much capital is needed to produce it without using principal?",
 "600,000 dollars", ["150,000 dollars", "1,500,000 dollars", "300,000 dollars"],
 "Capital needed = annual income / rate = 30,000 / 0.05 = 600,000. Spending principal as well would need less."),
("A capital needs analysis for a family usually starts by listing:",
 "Cash needs at death and income needs", ["The insured's credit score and debts", "The family's investment risk profile", "The cheapest insurer available"],
 "The analysis totals cash needs at death (final expenses, debts, taxes, an emergency fund) and the capital needed to replace income, then subtracts existing assets and coverage."),
("Two shareholders each own a policy on the other to fund their buy-sell agreement. This structure is called:",
 "Criss-cross insurance", ["Corporate redemption", "Split-dollar insurance", "Key person insurance"],
 "In a criss-cross (cross-purchase) arrangement each shareholder owns and pays for a policy on the other and uses the proceeds to buy the deceased's shares."),
("What is the main purpose of key person insurance?",
 "Protect the firm if a key person dies", ["Pay the key person a pension", "Fund the employees' group plan", "Replace the key person's pay for the family"],
 "The business owns the policy and is the beneficiary, so the proceeds cover lost profits, hiring and loan pressure after a key person dies."),
("A shareholders' agreement is funded by corporate-owned life insurance. A key advantage is that:",
 "Proceeds can flow out via the CDA", ["The premiums become deductible", "No written agreement is needed", "The deceased's estate pays no tax at all"],
 "The corporation receives the death benefit and can use the CDA credit to pay tax-free capital dividends as part of buying back the deceased's shares."),
("Which client most needs permanent rather than term coverage?",
 "One expecting a big tax bill at death", ["One with a 10-year car loan to repay", "One with young children for 15 years", "One with a mortgage to pay off in 20 years"],
 "Needs that last for life, such as capital gains tax at death or an estate gift, call for permanent coverage. Temporary needs fit term."),
("A policy owned by the insured and payable to her estate means the proceeds:",
 "Become part of the estate", ["Bypass the will", "Are protected from all creditors", "Go to her spouse automatically"],
 "Naming the estate brings the proceeds into the estate, where they can be subject to probate, the claims of creditors and the terms of the will."),
("A client wants to leave a set gift to a charity at death and get a tax receipt. One common approach is to:",
 "Name the charity as beneficiary", ["Name the charity as the life insured", "Cancel the policy and give the cash", "Make the charity a contingent owner only"],
 "A direct beneficiary designation to a registered charity gives the estate a donation receipt for the death benefit, which can offset tax on the final return."),
("When a life insured dies and the policy's owner is another living person, the policy:",
 "Pays the beneficiary the owner named", ["Passes to the life insured's estate", "Is taxed in the life insured's return", "Ends with no benefit paid to anyone"],
 "The owner controls the policy and names the beneficiary. The death of the life insured triggers the benefit, which is paid to that beneficiary."),
]
